-- title:
--   Normalisation of the j-line at level Nq: noetherian, normal, finite
-- statement:
--   Let $q$ be a prime, $N\ge 1$, let $A$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, let `dataNq` be modular polynomial data at level $Nq$ (a monic $\Phi\in\mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(Nq)$ annihilating the $q$-expansion $j_{Nq}$ over $j$), and let $K\subseteq\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ be an intermediate field finite over $\mathbb{Q}$. Write $R=$ `jRing A K` for the subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-Laurent series generated by the constant series attached to `coeffSubring A K` together with $j$, and $F=$ `fieldOver (N*q) K` for the subfield generated by the constant series from $K$ together with $j$ and $j_{Nq}$; let $C=$ `jIntegralClosure (N*q) A K` be the subring of elements of $F$ that are integral over $R$. The assertion is fourfold: $C$ is a noetherian ring; $C$ is integrally closed; every $x\in F$ admits $c\in C$ and $s\in R$ with $s\neq 0$ and $xs=c$; and there is a finite set $s\subseteq C$ such that every $x\in C$ is of the form $\sum_{c\in s}f(c)\,c$ for some function $f$ taking values in $R$ on $s$, i.e. $C$ is a finite $R$-module.
--
--   This is the finiteness and normality of the normalisation of the $j$-line at level $Nq$: the integral closure of $A_0[j]$ (with $A_0=A\cap K$) inside the field $K(j,j_{Nq})$ of $q$-expansions is noetherian, integrally closed, has $K(j,j_{Nq})$ as its field of fractions with denominators in $A_0[j]$, and is module-finite over $A_0[j]$, the standard consequence of finiteness of integral closure in a finite separable extension. It supplies the Dedekind-type base ring used by the place-specialisation and prolongation arguments at level $Nq$, in particular when places of $K(j,j_{Nq})$ are identified with height-one primes of this normalisation.
